WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.980
In "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever," the Talokanil
prove themselves to be not only skilled fighters

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.510
in the ocean, but also exceptional fighters
on land, even though they can't breathe air.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:14.150
This is made especially clear during Okoye's
fierce battle with Attuma on the bridge in

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.150
Boston.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.090
Generally speaking, the Dora Milaje wipe the
floor with all kinds of adversaries, including

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.090
super-soldiers.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.320
However, Attuma's victory over Okoye in their
first fight is shockingly decisive.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.160
Although she beats him in a rematch, the Talokans'
incredible skill is still firmly established.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.730
Namor's fighting abilities can be chalked
up to his status as an amphibious mutant,

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.380
but that isn't the case with his blue-skinned
people.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.160
So how did they become such good fighters
on dry land?

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.020
This is a people who have managed to stay
hidden from the surface world for centuries.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.219
Why are they so well prepared to take land-dwellers
on if, for generations, there's been no need

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:49.219
to do so?

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:53.039
The end of "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever"
implies we'll be seeing a lot more of Namor

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:54.660
and his people in the MCU.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.660
Maybe then we'll learn how these underwater
people got so good at fighting in the open

00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:58.910
air.

